# Untitled

Nanoparticles@nanoscale metal-organic framework composites as highly efficient heterogeneous catalysts for size- and shape-selective reactions
Bingqing Wang<sup>1</sup>, Wenxian Liu<sup>1</sup>, Weina Zhang<sup>2</sup>, and Junfeng Liu<sup>1</sup> (✉)
<sup>1</sup> State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, China  <sup>2</sup> Key Laboratory of Flexible Electronics (KLOFE) & Institute of Advanced Materials (IAM), Jiangsu National Synergetic Innovation Center for Advanced Materials (SICAM), Nanjing Tech University (Nanjing Tech), Nanjing 211816, China
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2017
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, nanoparticles, heterogeneous catalysts, selective catalysis
## ABSTRACT
Composites incorporating nanoparticles (NPs) within metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) find applications in many different fields. In particular, using MOF layers as molecular sieves built on the NPs could enable selectivity in heterogeneous catalysis. However, such composites typically exhibit low catalytic efficiency, due to the slow diffusion of the reactants in the long and narrow channels of the MOF shell. In order to improve the catalytic efficiency of these systems, here we report the fabrication of NPs incorporated in nanosized MOFs (NPs@nano-MOFs), obtained by reducing the size of the MOF crystals grown around the NPs. The crystal size of the composites was controlled by modulating the nucleation rate of the MOFs during the encapsulation of pre-synthesized and catalytically active NPs; in this way, NPs@MOF crystals smaller than 50 nm were synthesized and subsequently used as highly efficient catalysts. Due to the shorter path from the MOF surface to the active sites, the obtained Pt@nano-MOFs composites showed a higher conversion rate than their larger-sized counterparts in the synthesis of imines via cascade reaction of nitrobenzene and in the hydrogenation of olefins, while retaining the excellent size and shape selectivity associated with the molecular sieving effect of the MOF layer. The present strategy can also be applied to prepare other encapsulated nanostructures combining various types of NPs and nano-MOFs, thus highlighting the broad potential of this approach for developing optimized catalysts with high reactivity and selectivity.
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a unique class
of porous materials constructed from metal nodes and organic linkers. These compounds exhibit several remarkable properties, including large surface areas,
ordered crystalline pores, along with structural adaptivity and flexibility, which enable the incorporation of active phases supporting their use in catalytic applications [1–5]. In particular, heterogeneous catalysts composed of nanoparticles (NPs) incorporated in MOFs have attracted significant attention from the catalysis community [6–10]. As host matrices, the MOFs not only preserve the stability of the NPs but also provide outstanding size or shape selectivity in catalytic reactions [11–20]. The incorporation of NPs into MOFs can be achieved by either "ship-in-a-bottle" or "bottle-around-ship" approaches, through a two-step or a multi-step procedure [8]. The first method is based on the reduction of the doped precursors in the preformed MOFs, to produce NPs that are deposited in the cavities. Unfortunately, some NPs produced through this approach are inevitably deposited on the external surface of the MOF crystals, which limits the size and shape selectivity of the MOF-based catalysts. The second method, which involves the assembly of MOF precursors around pre-synthesized NPs, is a versatile and promising strategy for overcoming the intrinsic limitations of the first method. Nevertheless, most of the incorporated MOFs currently used in heterogeneous catalysis have sizes of the order of hundreds of nanometers or even several micrometers [15–17, 21, 22]. Despite their excellent catalytic selectivity, these MOFs have unfavorably long and narrow channels separating their surface from the active sites of the encapsulated NPs, which reduces the diffusion rates of the reactants and thus the catalytic efficiency. Therefore, the fabrication of NPs@MOF composites combining size selectivity and adequate efficiency for heterogeneous catalysis is a goal of high importance, which still represents a significant challenge at present.
Previous studies have shown that constructing mesoporous MOF (meso-MOF) structures around the NPs is an effective way to improve the diffusion rate of the reactants and the catalytic activity of NPs@MOF composites [23, 24]. For example, Pt@meso-MOF composites fabricated through selective etching of encapsulated NPs exhibited an enhanced conversion rate and good selectivity in the catalytic hydrogenation reaction [23]. Moreover, fabricating hollow MOFs around the NPs can also shorten the diffusion path of the reactants to the active sites and improve the
Previous studies have shown that constructing mesoporous MOF (meso-MOF) structures around the NPs is an effective way to improve the diffusion rate of the reactants and the catalytic activity of NPs@MOF composites [23, 24]. For example, Pt@meso-MOF composites fabricated through selective etching of encapsulated NPs exhibited an enhanced conversion rate and good selectivity in the catalytic hydrogenation reaction [23]. Moreover, fabricating hollow MOFs around the NPs can also shorten the diffusion path of the reactants to the active sites and improve the catalytic activity [12, 25–28]. Wang et al. succeeded in building NPs@hollow-MOF composites that exhibited enhanced catalytic activity in the selective hydrogenation of 1-chloro-2-nitrobenzene through a surface energy-driven solvothermal mechanism [28]; the improved catalytic activity of these composites was attributed to the cavities formed between the NPs and the MOF shell. Although the current fabrication methods exhibit clear advantages, they either require expensive and tedious processes or cannot be used to prepare different types of NPs@MOF composites, which greatly limits their applicability. Since molecular diffusion represents a key factor in the catalytic efficiency of NPs@MOFs, the synthesis of smaller MOF crystals containing NPs could bring substantial advantages. The fabrication of nanosized MOF crystals can shorten the diffusion path of the reactants and products to and from the catalytic sites, increasing the diffusion rate of the molecules involved in the catalytic process. Several approaches have been developed to synthesize nanoscale MOF (nano-MOF) crystals [29, 30]. For example, nano-MOFs can be synthesized using different capping reagents to modulate the coordination equilibria between the metal ions and the organic linkers and to regulate the rates of framework expansion and crystal growth [31–34]. Nano-MOFs have also been synthesized by adding excess ligands, which can function as linkers (in deprotonated form), growth terminators, and stabilizers (in neutral form) [35, 36]. The successful regulation of the MOF size enables the synthesis of highly efficient NPs@nano-MOF composites for heterogeneous catalysis. However, despite these potential benefits, the incorporation of NPs in nanoscale MOFs has rarely been reported.
In this work, we report the fabrication of NPs@nano-MOF catalysts with high efficiency and selectivity (Scheme 1). NPs@MOF crystals as small as \(50\,\mathrm{nm}\) were synthesized by regulating the nucleation and growth of the MOFs around pre-synthesized catalytically-active NPs. This strategy is suitable for synthesizing a variety of NPs and MOF crystals: For instance, we show that well-dispersed Pt or Pd NPs can be incorporated in several types of nanosized MOFs (UiO-66, UiO-66-NH\(_2\), MOF-801, ZIF-8, ZIF-7, and ZnCo-ZIF-67). The NPs@nano-MOFs, with highly active NPs sites and short molecular diffusion paths,
exhibit good catalytic selectivity due to the molecular sieving effect of the MOF layer and the enhanced rate of the catalytic reactions involved in the selectivity mechanism. The Pt@nano-MOFs composites showed a higher conversion rate than their larger-sized counterparts in the cascade synthesis of imines from nitrobenzene and in the hydrogenation of olefins, while retaining their excellent size and shape selectivity in the catalytic hydrogenation processes.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Preparation of NPs@MOFs
Pt@nano-UiO-66, Pt@nano-UiO-66-NH\(_2\), and Pt@nano-MOF-801 composites were synthesized using acetic acid as modulator. Typically, for the synthesis of Pt@nano-UiO-66, 4 mL of a \(N,N-\) dimethylformamide (DMF) solution of \(\mathrm{ZnCl}_{4}\) (36 mM) and 4 mL of a DMF solution of terephthalic acid (BDC, 38 mM) were mixed in a glass vial. Subsequently, 0.5 mL of acetic acid and 2 mL of a DMF solution of Pt NPs were added to the glass vial, and the reaction was then performed at 120 °C for 24 h without stirring. The product was collected by centrifugation and then washed three times with DMF. After soaking the product in methanol at 40 °C for 12 h and washing three times with methanol to replace the soaking solvent, the Pt@nano-UiO-66 composites were activated at 50 °C after solvent removal by vacuum evaporation for 5 h. Larger-sized Pt@UiO-66 composites with crystal sizes of \(\sim125\) and 380 nm were prepared by the procedure described above, with the only exception that 1.5 and 2.5 mL acetic acid were used, respectively, and that the Pt NPs were added
The Pt@nano-ZIF-8, Pt@nano-ZIF-7, and Pt@nano-ZnCo-ZIF-67 composites were synthesized by adding excess ligands as modulators. Typically, for the synthesis of Pt@nano-ZIF-8, a solution of \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) (55 mM) in 4.5 mL of methanol was poured rapidly into a solution of 2-methylimidazole (Hmim, 439 mM) in 4.5 mL of methanol under magnetic stirring. Then, 1 mL of a methanol solution of Pt NPs was added, and the mixture slowly turned turbid. After 1 h, the nanocrystals were separated from the dispersion by centrifugation, washed with fresh methanol, and dried at 40 °C under vacuum. Larger-size Pt@ZIF-8 (850 nm) composites were prepared by following a similar procedure, except that 55 mM Hmim and 1 mL of Pt NPs methanol solution were used. Pt@nano-ZIF-7 and Pt@nano-ZnCo-ZIF-7 were also prepared by similar procedures, whose details are reported in the ESM.
Pd@nano-MOFs composites were synthesized by a similar procedure to that used for the Pt@nano-MOFs ones, by replacing the Pt NPs solution with a solution of Pd nanocubes (NCs).
## 2.2 Catalytic tests
Cascade reductive imination of nitrobenzene was carried out to test the catalytic activity of the systems prepared in the previous step. First, the catalyst (containing 0.23 mg Pt, detected by inductively coupled plasma, ICP-MS) was added in a 25 mL round flask, and the residual air in the reactor was expelled by flushing it several times with hydrogen. Then, 2 mL of ethanol was added in the flask, and the mixture was sonicated for several minutes to form a homogeneous suspension. 22 mL nitrobenzene (0.2 mM) and 35 mL benzaldehyde (0.3 mM) were then added in the flask, followed again by removal of the air in the reactor. The reaction mixture was stirred at 30 °C under 1 bar of H\(_2\). The progress of the reaction was monitored by
gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS, Agilent, HP-5 capillary column). The conversion rate was defined as the mole ratio of converted to initial nitrobenzene. Catalytic hydrogenation of alkenes was carried out with a similar procedure, whose details are reported in the ESM, which also includes additional information about the materials employed and the synthesis of Pt and Pd NPs, as well as other experimental details.
## 3 Results and discussion
The formation of MOF crystals in solution follows the nucleation of multidentate ligands and metal ions [37, 38]. The properties of the MOF crystals, such as size, morphology, and crystallinity can be controlled by modulating the interactions (or "coordination equilibria") between metal ions and organic linkers. In particular, the crystal size can be regulated through the nucleation or growth rate of the crystal [37]. UiO-66, which is a carboxylate-based MOF with large surface area and high physicochemical stability [39], was used as a model to demonstrate the fabrication of NPs@nano-MOFs. Typically, UiO-66 crystals are synthesized by a modulator-based strategy. In this process, capping reagents (modulators), which have only one coordination site but share the same chemical functionality as the linkers, are used to reduce the nucleation rate. The competition from the modulator is used to selectively modulate the coordination equilibria during the nucleation and crystal growth processes, providing an effective control over the size of the UiO-66 crystals. Here, we demonstrate that the crystal size of Pt@UiO-66 composites can be reduced to \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\) using acetic acid as the modulator, due to the competitive interaction of dicarboxylic linkers and acetic acid molecules with the zirconium ions during the nucleation and crystal growth processes. Typically, a specific amount of acetic acid and uniformly sized, pre-synthesized polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP)-modified Pt NPs of \(3\pm0.3\,\mathrm{nm}\) size (Fig. S1(a) in the ESM) was introduced in a mixed solution of \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) and BDC in DMF, and the resulting solution was kept at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) without stirring. The reaction solution turned cloudy after \(3\,\mathrm{h}\), and the supernatant was transparent and colorless after \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), which indicated that almost
all NPs had been incorporated in the composites. To obtain the Pt@nano-UiO-66 composites, an optimized amount \((0.5\,\mathrm{mL})\) of acetic acid was used in the reaction, with zirconium ions/BDC/acetic acid molar ratios of 1:1:60. The morphology and structural features of the as-prepared Pt@nano-UiO-66 composites were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). As shown in Fig. 1(a), partly due to above-mentioned competitive interaction of the dicarboxylic linkers and acetic acid molecules with the zirconium ions during nucleation and crystal growth, nearly all the Pt NPs were encapsulated within the MOFs after the reaction, and a mean crystal size of \(\sim30\,\mathrm{nm}\) was measured for the Pt@UiO-66 composite. Additional tilting experiments were carried out to verify the encapsulation of the Pt NPs in the MOF nanocrystals. Figure S2 in the ESM shows a series of TEM pictures taken under tilting angles ranging from \(-30^{\circ}\) to \(+30^{\circ}\). No Pt NPs were observed at the edges of the MOF crystals during the whole tilting process, confirming that the majority of the Pt NPs are located inside the nano-UiO-66 structure. Increasing the modulator concentration reduced the nucleation rate of the MOFs and resulted in larger NPs@MOF crystals. Thus, Pt@UiO-66 composites with a crystal size of \(\sim125\) and \(\sim380\,\mathrm{nm}\) were obtained using 1:5 and \(2.5\,\mathrm{mL}\) of acetic acid, with BDC/ acetic acid molar ratios of 1:180 and 1:290, respectively (Fig. S3 in the ESM).
When the reaction was carried out without the modulator, rapid nucleation resulted in aggregation and poor crystallinity, with the formation of an ill-defined gel-like morphology (Fig. S4 in the ESM) [32]. In addition, the crystal structure of the Pt@UiO-66 composites was characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD). The presence of sharp diffraction lines clearly indicates that the UiO-66 crystalline structure was maintained (Fig. 1(g)). No peaks corresponding to Pt NPs were observed, which is mainly attributed to the low loading and high dispersion of the ultra-small Pt NPs inside the MOF matrix.
This modulator-regulated encapsulation strategy can be successfully extended to other types of MOFs, such as UiO-66-NH\(_2\) (Zr\(_6\)O\(_4\)(OH)\(_4\)(NH\(_2\)-BDC)\(_6\)) and MOF-801 (Zr\(_6\)O\(_4\)(OH)\(_4\)(fumarate)\(_6\)) [40, 41]. The TEM images (Figs. 1(b) and 1(c)) clearly show that the acetic acid modulator can be used to limit the crystal size of
the \(\mathrm{Pt@UiO-66-NH_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt@MOF-801}\) composites to \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\). This strategy was also applied to other types of NPs with different sizes and shapes. For example, Pd NCs \((8\,\pm\,1\,\mathrm{nm}\), Fig. S1(b) in the ESM) can be encapsulated in UiO-66, UiO-66-NH\(_2\), and MOF-801 through this strategy (Figs. 1(d)–1(f)), demonstrating its versatility. The similarity of the experimental and simulated XRD peaks (Figs. 1(g)–1(i)) highlights the high crystallinity of the NPs@nano-MOFs.
Moreover, NPs@nano-ZIF-8 composites can be obtained by precisely controlling the concentration of organic ligands at the beginning of the MOF crystal nucleation.
ZIF-8 belongs to a subclass of MOFs (zeolitic imidazolate frameworks, ZIFs) used in a wide range of
applications, such as gas storage [42], gas separation, and heterogeneous catalysis [43]. The conventional methods for preparing ZIF-8 often result in the formation of large (several micrometers) ZIF-8 crystals [43]. However, nanoscale ZIF-8 crystals can also be synthesized by adding an excess of the bridging ligand [35]. Therefore, we successfully prepared NPs@nano-ZIF-8 composites from a solution containing an excess of bridging ligand to stabilize the newly formed nuclei and terminate the crystal growth of the MOFs. Typically, the Pt@nano-ZIF-8 composites were synthesized by adding pre-synthesized PVP-modified Pt NPs into a methanol solution of \(\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}\) and excess Hmim (with a \(\mathrm{Hmim/Zn(II)}\) molar ratio of 8:1) and keeping the resulting solution at room
temperature for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) under stirring. In the reaction solution, Hmim can act as a linker unit in its deprotonated form or a stabilizing unit in its neutral form [35]. Excess Hmim in its neutral form will terminate the crystal growth and stabilize the positively charged nanocrystals. As shown in Fig. 2(a), the Pt@nano-ZIF-8 composite had a mean size of \(\sim40\,\mathrm{nm}\) and almost all the Pt NPs were encapsulated within the MOFs after the reaction. Pt@ZIF-8 composites with a larger crystal size were obtained by reducing the concentration of the ligand, with a \(1{:}1\ \mathrm{Hmim/Zn(II)}\) molar ratio (Fig. S7(a) in the ESM). However, no obvious changes were observed in the size of the composites upon a further increase in the concentration of Hmim (for a \(\mathrm{Hmim/Zn(II)}\) molar ratio \(>8{:}1\)). In
addition, several types of NPs@nano-MOFs composites with crystal sizes lower than \(50\;\mathrm{nm}\), such as Pd@nano-ZIF-8, Pt/Pd@nano-ZIF-7, and Pt/Pd@nano-ZnCo-ZIF-67 (Figs. 2(b)–2(f)), can be synthesized using this controlled process. The design and strategies for the synthesis of different NPs@nano-MOFs presented here thus offer a promising avenue for the development of highly efficient heterogeneous catalysts.
The NPs@nano-MOFs provide a short diffusion path for reactants and products to access and leave the active sites, which is expected to result in enhanced catalytic activity. The synthesis of imines from nitrobenzene was used as a model reaction to investigate the effect of the size of the NPs@MOFs and compare the catalytic efficiency of the NPs@nano-MOFs to
that of their larger counterparts. Due to their good electrophilicity, imines play an important role in various organic reactions, such as reduction, addition, and condensation [14]. Figure 3(a) illustrates the reduction process of nitrobenzene to aniline, followed by in situ condensation with benzaldehyde, leading to the formation of the final imines through a cascade-type reaction. In this study, we investigated the catalytic behavior of three \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) catalysts with different MOF sizes (30, 125, and \(380\,\mathrm{nm}\) ) in the above cascade reaction (Fig. 3(b), \(\sim10\,\mathrm{mg}\) catalyst, \(\sim2.3\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\) Pt). For comparison, Pt-free nanosized UiO-66 and Pt/nanoUiO-66, in which the Pt NPs are distributed at the outer surface of nanosized UiO-66, were also synthesized. As shown in Fig. 3(c), as the size of the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) composites decreased, the conversion rate of the reactants increased.
\(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) composites of \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\) size \((\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}-66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm})\) exhibited higher catalytic activity compared to the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}125\,\mathrm{nm}\) or \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}380\,\mathrm{nm}\) catalysts. The pure nano-UiO-66 exhibited no catalytic activity in the cascade reaction, which indicates that the catalytic activity of the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) composites is due to the Pt NPs embedded in the MOF matrix. The improved catalytic activity of the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) composites is primarily due to the shorter diffusion path from the substrate to the active sites. The active NP sites of the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm}\) catalysts are extremely
close to the crystal surface, which facilitates transfer of reactants and products and results in the highest catalytic activity observed for these composites [11]. Notably, the total conversion rate of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm}\) is very close to that of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{nano}-\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) (Fig. S5 in the ESM), which confirms the small diffusion barriers in \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm}\). However, the catalytic yield of the target product imines obtained with \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{nano}-\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) is lower than that obtained with \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm}\). This effect can be ascribed to the further hydrogenation of product imines to form \(N\)-phenylbenzylamine in the case of \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{nano}-\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\), whereas the same hydrogenation reaction is hindered when the Pt NPs are encapsulated within the MOF [14]. In addition, the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm}\) composite can easily be recycled from the reaction solution by centrifugation, ethanol-washing, and drying under vacuum.
The stability of a catalyst is another important factor to consider. To verify the stability of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\sim}30\,\mathrm{nm}\), the catalytic cascade reaction was repeated five times. As shown in Fig. S6(a) (in the ESM), the catalyst did not show any significant loss of activity after successive reuses. The phase and morphology of the reused catalyst remained virtually unchanged relative to that of the fresh catalyst (Figs. S6(b) and S6(c) in the ESM), further confirming its stability.
The enhanced catalytic activity and molecular sieving performance of the NPs@nano-MOFs was further demonstrated by examining the liquid-phase hydrogenation of \(n\)-hexene and cis-cyclooctene using \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) composites of \(40\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(850\,\mathrm{nm}\), and \(1.5\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) size containing \(0.2\,\mathrm{mg}\) Pt (Figs. 2(a) and S7 in the ESM), denoted as \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}40\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}850\,\mathrm{nm}\), and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}1.5\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\), respectively, as catalysts (Fig. 4(a)). As shown in Fig. 4(b), \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}850\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}1.5\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\), and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}40\,\mathrm{nm}\) were unable to catalyze the hydrogenation reaction of cyclooctene because this molecule (5.5 Å diameter) cannot penetrate through the small pores of ZIF-8 (3.4 Å). In contrast, all three \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\) composites exhibited catalytic activity for the hydrogenation of linear \(n\)-hexene, which demonstrates the excellent size selectivity achieved through the MOF layer of \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\). Moreover, the hydrogenation of linear \(n\)-hexene catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}850\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8{\sim}1.5\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) showed low conversion percentages
of \(23.8\%\) and \(7.6\%\), respectively, as a result of the long diffusive path through the small pores of ZIF-8. On the other hand, the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}{\sim}40\ \mathrm{nm}\) exhibited a significantly higher conversion rate \((95.3\%)\) after \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\). These results further indicate that the nano-MOFs provide a shorter diffusion path for the molecules, which facilitates their catalytic transformations. In addition, the \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}{\sim}40\ \mathrm{nm}\) catalysts exhibited good reusability, with similar conversions obtained in five consecutive runs of the hydrogenation reaction of \(n\)-hexene (Fig. 4(c)). No obvious difference was observed between the TEM images and XRD patterns of the freshly prepared and reused \(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{ZIF-8}{\sim}40\ \mathrm{nm}\) composites (Fig. S8 in the ESM), which indicates that the structural integrity of the composite was preserved.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have demonstrated a controlled strategy for the preparation of NPs@nano-MOF composites, based on modulating the nucleation of the MOFs during the encapsulation process. As host matrices, the nanoscale MOFs provided a shorter diffusion path and led to excellent size selectivity and higher catalytic activity of the Pt@nano-MOF composites compared with conventional, larger-sized Pt@MOFs. This strategy can be applied to the preparation of other encapsulated nanostructures combining various types of NPs and nano-MOFs, which highlights its broad potential for developing optimized catalysts with high reactivity and selectivity.
